{
 "id": "2022-urakami-flowers",
 "name": "Flowers, nature and flower drawings: emotional ratings",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the online survey in Urakami, Huss, Nagamine, Czamanski-Cohen & Zaccai (2022), \"The Emotional Experience of Flowers: Zoomed In, Zoomed Out and Painted\", Horticulturae 8(7):668 (253 adults from Europe, North America, Israel and Japan, spring 2020). After background questions, three statements on social distancing and a rating of their current mood, participants see 15 images one at a time in random order: 6 flower photos (dahlia, hydrangea, lisianthus, marguerite, cosmos, leopard plant), 6 nature scenes from Japan and Israel, and 3 drawings of flowers. For each they rate familiarity, liking and how the image makes them feel (unpleasant–pleasant, calm–excited, tired–awake) on 7-point scales, write free associations and pick one keyword (inspiring, annoying, boring, peaceful, pretty). The paper found flower photos liked more and felt more pleasant than nature scenes, nature scenes more exciting, and drawings less positive than photos. Images are the 15 survey images printed in the paper's Figure 1 (CC BY 4.0), used with credit; the template is removed if the authors object.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) the original SurveyMonkey questionnaire is no longer reachable, so question wording, scale end labels and the answer options for education and socio-economic status are reconstructed from the Methods section; (2) the images are cut from Figure 1 (about 410 px wide) and trimmed on the right to remove the panel letters; (3) a consent tick box is added and the free association is optional; (4) the drawings are named lotus, Ornithogalum and sunflowers as in the paper's caption.",
